WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.000
Federated learning.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.233
How to train one shared model when nobody is allowed to share their data.

3
00:00:07.433 --> 00:00:12.333
Federated learning trains a shared model across devices or organizations.

4
00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:14.733
Without pooling the data in one place.

5
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:19.267
It suits hospitals, phones, and companies whose contracts forbid sharing.

6
00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:22.033
Here's the FedAvg loop.

7
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:25.700
The server broadcasts the current global weights to every client.

8
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.067
Each client trains locally on its own private data for a few epochs.

9
00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:33.267
Clients send back only weight updates, never data.

10
00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:38.462
The server averages them, weighted by data size, into a new global model.

11
00:00:38.462 --> 00:00:39.267
Then repeat.

12
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:42.467
Compare it with the usual approach.

13
00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:44.867
Normally, data moves to the model.

14
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:46.967
Here, the model moves to the data.

15
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:48.733
Only updates come back.

16
00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:52.067
So raw data stays where the privacy rules need it.

17
00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:54.500
Here it is by hand.

18
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.333
Three clients each train a copy of the model on their own data.

19
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:02.900
Then, for every weight tensor, we stack the three versions and take the mean.

20
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.033
Load that average, and you have the new global model.

21
00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:08.533
Here's a real story.

22
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:15.067
Three ground station partners hold telemetry they can't share, but all want a better anomaly detector.

23
00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:23.033
You simulate it first: split your data three ways, each seeing different orbital regimes, and run five FedAvg rounds.

24
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:29.463
Federated lands within two points of centralized, and eleven above any single station's own model.

25
00:01:29.463 --> 00:01:33.467
That notebook turns a compliance stalemate into a collaboration.

26
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.133
There are honest caveats.

27
00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:39.919
Each client's data differs, so naive averaging drifts.

28
00:01:39.919 --> 00:01:44.567
Communication is the bottleneck, and updates can still leak information.

29
00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:49.667
Real deployments add differential privacy or secure aggregation on top.

30
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:51.570
Let's recap.

31
00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.033
Train a shared model without pooling data.

32
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:58.667
FedAvg broadcasts, trains locally, sends updates, and averages.

33
00:01:58.967 --> 00:02:02.300
Watch for non-IID drift and communication cost.

34
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.832
And add privacy protections on top.

35
00:02:04.832 --> 00:02:07.000
Next up: quantum machine learning.
